\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Panels (a)--(f) correspond, respectively, to $k=0$, $1.5$, $2$, $2.05$, $2.5$, and $4$; the Hopf value is $k_H=2$. The finite equilibrium $E_*=(1,1-k)$ is a stable node in (a), a stable focus in (b), a stable weak focus in (c), an unstable focus in (d)--(e), and an unstable node in (f). Filled and open circles denote attracting and repelling equilibria, respectively. The two horizontal boundary points are source nodes, whereas the vertical diamonds denote degenerate boundary saddles, each with two incoming boundary separatrices, one outgoing interior separatrix, and two hyperbolic sectors on the disk side. Blue curves are numerical approximations of the outgoing interior separatrices from infinity; gray curves are sample trajectories, and arrows indicate forward time. Red curves in (d)--(f) are numerically computed attracting periodic orbits; panel (d) illustrates a parameter value near the Hopf threshold. The inset in (f) magnifies the indicated lower-boundary region and distinguishes the periodic orbit from the blue separatrix and the disk boundary. The interior trajectories were obtained by direct numerical integration. }}{25}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hr-representative-portraits}{{1}{25}{Panels (a)--(f) correspond, respectively, to $k=0$, $1.5$, $2$, $2.05$, $2.5$, and $4$; the Hopf value is $k_H=2$. The finite equilibrium $E_*=(1,1-k)$ is a stable node in (a), a stable focus in (b), a stable weak focus in (c), an unstable focus in (d)--(e), and an unstable node in (f). Filled and open circles denote attracting and repelling equilibria, respectively. The two horizontal boundary points are source nodes, whereas the vertical diamonds denote degenerate boundary saddles, each with two incoming boundary separatrices, one outgoing interior separatrix, and two hyperbolic sectors on the disk side. Blue curves are numerical approximations of the outgoing interior separatrices from infinity; gray curves are sample trajectories, and arrows indicate forward time. Red curves in (d)--(f) are numerically computed attracting periodic orbits; panel (d) illustrates a parameter value near the Hopf threshold. The inset in (f) magnifies the indicated lower-boundary region and distinguishes the periodic orbit from the blue separatrix and the disk boundary. The interior trajectories were obtained by direct numerical integration}{figure.1}{}}
